./xmodel/ideation-20260831T1033Z-crosspoll-sol56.log:429:    77	For a surviving rank-four candidate, homogenize the proposed irreducible `A1`-normalized one-place curve to $\bar B\subset\mathbb{CP}^2$. When every singularity of $\bar B$ is unibranch, the candidate is a rational cuspidal plane curve; its projective degree and local value semigroups must satisfy the Heegaard–Floer correction-term inequalities of Borodzik–Livingston and their arbitrary-genus extension. These are effective inequalities on semigroup-counting functions, so the delta-sequence compiler can test them by integer arithmetic. The primary sources are Borodzik–Livingston, *Heegaard Floer homology and rational cuspidal curves*, Forum Math. Sigma 2 (2014), e28, [arXiv:1304.1062](https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.1062), and Borodzik–Hedden–Livingston, *Plane algebraic curves of arbitrary genus via Heegaard Floer homology*, Comment. Math. Helv. 92 (2017), 215–256, [doi:10.4171/CMH/411](https://doi.org/10.4171/CMH/411).
./xmodel/ideation-20260831T1033Z-crosspoll-sol56.log:433:    81	For noncuspidal controls, Borodzik–Liu–Zemke construct link-Floer/knotification obstructions and explicitly treat transverse double points in *Heegaard Floer homology, knotifications of links, and plane curves with noncuspidal singularities*, Algebr. Geom. Topol. 24 (2024), 4837–4889, [doi:10.2140/agt.2024.24.4837](https://doi.org/10.2140/agt.2024.24.4837). That paper licenses a node branch of the compiler and a regression against the promoted one-node kill; it does **not** by itself license arbitrary tangencies. A tangential or general multibranch record therefore returns typed `OPEN` until its exact link-cobordism hypotheses are proved.
