./xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:98:**Construction.** Same normal form as §2. One additional odd vanishing `h_{11}=0`, with `h_9≠0`. Relative to `I_21` this is a hypersurface. The even-reduced slice of §2 already has `h_{12}≠0` at the finite points with `h_{15}=h_{13}=0`; that still does not constrain `h_{11}`. No square-ansatz identity is desk-visible: `c=9` would require an element of degree 9 in `ℂ[p,q]`, which is already in the numerical semigroup as `9=9` (primitive), not a polynomial in `r` of degree 3.
./xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:109:**Construction.** One further odd vanishing `h_9=0`, open `h_7≠0`. The semigroup is generated by 6,7,8; a degree-7 element is primitive. The square ansatz `q=r^2` with `deg r=3` produces `S⊇⟨3,8⟩` of genus 7, which is *smaller* `δ_aff` than 9 and a *larger* `β_1` (the next type). Specialising from `c=7` to `c=3` by forcing `r∈ℂ[p,q]` is a closed condition, not a construction of the present type.
./xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:122:**Sourced lead, not a kill.** Fujimoto–Suzuki–Yokoyama, *On polynomial curves in the affine plane*, Osaka J. Math. **43** (2006), 597–608, p. 602: “Moh [11] showed that there is no polynomial curve with the `δ`-sequence `{6,8,3}`.” Their convention (Theorem 2: `δ_0<δ_1`; Fact 4: `deg_t x=deg_y f`) makes `{6,8,3}` exactly the present type after swapping coordinates (`x=q` degree 6, `y=p` degree 8, extra generator 3). They note that the *semigroup* `⟨6,8,3⟩=⟨3,8⟩` *is* realised by `(t^3,t^8)`, whose `δ`-sequence is `{3,8}`, not `{6,8,3}`. The citation is Moh, *On the Jacobian conjecture and the configurations of roots*, J. Reine Angew. Math. **340** (1983), 140–212. That PDF was not obtained (GDZ/EUDML endpoints did not yield article bytes). A secondary one-line citation, without Moh’s argument on disk, is not a sourced obstruction in the sense of this lane: it is a lead. The same FSY paper *does* give a complete Gröbner-basis non-realisation of `{6,21,4}` and `{6,22,17}` by their Lemma 1 (the second approximate root `g_2(x(t),y(t))` must have `t`-degree `δ_2`); that method applies verbatim here and is the CAS job below.
./xmodel/nodal-realization-86-96-grok46-20260831.md:137:**Admissibility.** `Δ=(9,6,4)`, `n_1=n_2=3`, `3·6=18∈⟨9⟩`, `3·4=12=2·6∈⟨9,6⟩`, `4<18`, `3∤4`. Gaps of `⟨4,6,9⟩` are the six listed in §1.2. Germ `(3;23)`, `δ_∞=22`, cluster `(3^7,2,1^2)` summing to 25, `M_∞=25>24`.
