./xmodel/pi1s4-64-fold-reduction-hostile-review-grok46-20260831.md:50:**Repair.** The charged converse — “`δ_aff=3` at `(d,n)=(6,4)` forces `Γ=⟨3,4⟩` (the only 3-gap subsemigroup of `ℕ` containing `⟨6,4⟩`)” — is **false** as semigroup theory. The complete list of genus-3 numerical semigroups is four:
./xmodel/pi1s4-64-fold-reduction-hostile-review-grok46-20260831.md:52:| semigroup | gaps | contains `⟨4,6⟩`? | symmetric? |
./xmodel/pi1s4-64-fold-reduction-hostile-review-grok46-20260831.md:59:Three of them contain `⟨4,6⟩`. The parenthetical is therefore not a proof. What *does* isolate `⟨3,4⟩` in this lane is the δ-sequence calculus: the only length-3 sequence `Δ=(6,4,c)` with `c` odd, `c<6`, and `δ_aff=3` is `c=3` (the competitors `c=5` and `c=1` give genus 4 and 0 respectively). Equivalently: the affine coordinate ring is a plane complete intersection, hence Gorenstein, so `Γ` is symmetric; the only symmetric 3-gap semigroups are `⟨3,4⟩` and `⟨2,7⟩`; and `2∈Γ` would put a degree-2 element in `C[p,q]`, whence `C(p,q)⊆C(ρ)` with `[C(t):C(ρ)]=2`, contradicting birationality of a genuine `(6,4)` type. Either of those repairs is acceptable; the charged sentence is not.
./xmodel/pi1s4-64-fold-reduction-hostile-review-grok46-20260831.md:61:**Lemma 1.2, re-derived.** `Γ=⟨3,4⟩` supplies `ρ∈C[p,q]` with `deg ρ=3`. Then `C[ρ,q]⊆C[p,q]`. The coprime pair `(deg ρ,deg q)=(3,4)` has value semigroup `⟨3,4⟩` (the unique relation is at degree 12, `ρ⁴∼q³`, and does not fill `{1,2,5}`). Equal filtered semigroups inside `C[t]` plus inclusion give equality: given `h` in the larger ring of `t`-degree `n`, take `h′` in the smaller with the same leading term, and induct on `deg(h−h′)`. The rings are filtered, not graded; leading-term subtraction is still valid. So `C[p,q]=C[ρ,q]`.
./xmodel/pi1s4-64-fold-reduction-hostile-review-grok46-20260831.md:79:For `Δ=(8,4,6,3)` the semigroup is `⟨8,4,6,3⟩=⟨3,4⟩`, so again `C[P,q]=C[r,q]` with `deg r=3`. Reduce `i≤3`. The pairs with `3i+4j≤8` are `(0,0),(1,0),(2,0),(0,1),(1,1),(0,2)`; injectivity again forbids cancellation, and the unique degree-8 pair is `(0,2)`. Hence
