./xmodel/round1033-budget-n-gpt55-20260831.log:226:86:5. **Degree-monotone invariant, if one exists.** Adjacent colourable survivors at conductors 12/14/18/20/24/26 are a promoted existence proof that the ladder is not monotone. A new invariant must come from algebra (semigroup, residual cover, dicritical remainder), not from more even conductors.
./xmodel/round1033-budget-n-gpt55-20260831.log:236:115:- `(2,1,1)`: degree 2, either two sections or a connected étale double cover of `B^sm`. Two sections reduce to the `(3,1)` extension problem with multiplicity two. A connected double cover of `A1 minus s` punctures is classified by the subset of punctures of odd ramification (plus possibly infinity). The numerical semigroup of a one-place curve determines those punctures (Assi–García-Sánchez, arXiv:1407.0490: a δ-sequence determines the semigroup, the conductor, and the affine singularities). A colourable survivor whose semigroup cannot carry either two sections or such a double cover is combinatorially unrealizable as `A_F`, even though it is colourable as a braid. Which of the two topologies occurs is `OPEN` until typed; do not fill by cap.
./xmodel/round1033-budget-n-gpt55-20260831.log:237:119:**Primary literature.** Chau, *Two remarks on non-zero constant Jacobian polynomial maps of `C^2`*, Ann. Polon. Math. 82.1 (2003), Lemma 5: `A_F` equals the union of images of dicritical series. Orevkov 1987, (4) and Cor. 4.3, used only as already typed at plane rank four (no export). Assi–García-Sánchez, *Algorithms for curves with one place at infinity*, J. Symbolic Comput. / arXiv:1407.0490, for the semigroup-to-punctures dictionary. Galindo–Monserrat–Moreno-Ávila–Moyano-Fernández, *On δ-sequences and surfaces at infinity*, arXiv:2408.15931, for the dual graph of the resolution at infinity from a δ-sequence. Neumann–Rudolph unfolding and Neumann's minimal-Seifert theorem as already consumed by the promoted one-node packet (C1 mandatory).
./xmodel/round1033-budget-n-gpt55-20260831.log:262:231:**Cheapest discriminator.** Partition the finite list of colourable surviving δ-sequences into `(3,1)`-capable and `(2,1,1)`-capable using only the already-computed colouring (cycle types of meridians). For one cheapest `(3,1)` sequence: decide, from the semigroup alone, whether a degree-1 section of a finite map over `B^sm` can extend across every affine singularity. For one cheapest `(2,1,1)` sequence: list the punctures of `B^sm` from the δ-sequence and decide whether the residual degree-2 map can exist either as two sections or as a connected double cover (a homomorphism `pi1(B^sm) -> Z/2` whose ramification support is compatible with a generic `(2,1,1)` fibre). Do not assume connectedness. Desk-scale; no CAS; no compactification of a Keller pair.
