62-contributions in this direction are the following. For (2) and (3) we make extensive use of the
63:concept of δ-sequence (see Page 7).
64-    (1) (Section 3) Given a polynomial f ∈ K[x, y], decide if f has one place at infinity and if
65-        yes, compute the set of its approximate roots and the generators of its semigroup. Our
66-        approach here is the irreducibility criterion given by S.S. Abhyankar in [4]. This criterion is
67-        straightforward from the equation. It is based on the notion of generalized Newton polygons
68-        and it does not use neither the resolution of singularities nor the calculation of Puiseux series
69-        at infinity. We have implemented this procedure using the numericalsgps ([9]) GAP ([11])
70-        package, and named it SemigroupOfValuesOfPlaneCurveWithSinglePlaceAtInfinity.
71-    (2) (Section 4) Given a sequence of integers in N, decide if this sequence generates the semigroup
--
75-        this is CurveAssociatedToDeltaSequence (compare with [10, Algorithm 1]).
76-    (3) (Section 5) Given a positive integer g, compute the set of semigroups of polynomials with
77-        one place at infinity with genus g (hence with conductor 2g). Note that if Γ is such a
78-        semigroup and if f is a polynomial with one place at infinity whose semigroup is Γ, then g
79-        is the geometric genus of a nonsingular element of the pencil (f − λ)λ∈K , and 2g is the rank
80-        of the K-vector space K[x, y]/(fx , fy ), where fx (respectively fy ) denotes the x-derivative
81-        (respectively the y-derivative) of f . The function DeltaSequencesWithFrobeniusNumber
82-        has been designed to do this. In [10] there is an alternative procedure to compute all
83:        δ-sequences with given genus.
84-   All these GAP functions will be included in the next release of the package numericalsgps.
85-   In Section 1 we recall the main properties of curves with one place at infinity and we give a proof
86-of Abhyanlar-Moh Theorem: a rational nonsingular one place curve is equivalent to a coordinate of
87-K2 . In Section 2 we explicitly describe the automorphism that transforms the equation of the curve
88-into a coordinate. The method of the proof allows us to give a simple demonstration of the fact
89:that two equivalent polynomials with one place at infinity have the same δ-sequences. In the last
90-section, we study the embedding of polynomial curves (rational curves with one place at infinity).
91-In particular, we give an example of a polynomial curve with two inequivalent embeddings in the
92-affine plane.
